=3D"style_learnContent__K5K7M"><header><h1>Maximum Sum of a Continuous Path=
 in a Binary Tree</h1></header><div class=3D"style_problemDescription__T4cP=
e"><p>Return the <strong>maximum sum of a continuous path</strong> in a bin=
ary tree. A path is defined by the following characteristics:</p><ul>
<li>Consists of a sequence of nodes that can begin and end at any node in t=
he tree.</li>
<li>Each consecutive pair of nodes in the sequence is connected by an edge.=
</li>
<li>The path must be a <strong>single continuous sequence</strong> of nodes=
 that doesn't split into multiple paths.</li>
</ul><h4 id=3D"example">Example:</h4><figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"d=
isplay: flex; justify-content: center;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a bina=
ry tree structure where nodes contain numerical values.  The root node, at =
the top, is labeled '5'.  This root node branches into two child nodes: '-1=
0' on the left and '8' on the right. The '-10' node further branches into '=
1' and '-7', which in turn branch into '11' and '-1' respectively. The '8' =
node branches into '9' and '7'. The '7' node further branches into '6' and =
'-3'.  A peach-colored highlight encompasses the nodes '8', '9', '7', '6', =
and '-3'. To the right of this highlighted section, a calculation is shown:=
 'sum =3D 9 + 8 + 7 + 6 =3D 30', indicating a summation of the values withi=
n the highlighted subtree.  The entire diagram illustrates a tree data stru=
cture and a specific calculation performed on a subset of its nodes." width=
=3D"471" height=3D"205" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://b=
ytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/trees/maximum-sum-of-a-continu=
ous-path-in-a-binary-tree/maximum-sum-of-a-continuous-path-FNI3CLNH.svg" st=
yle=3D"color: transparent;"></div></figure><pre><code class=3D"hljs languag=
e-python">Output: <span class=3D"hljs-number">30</span>
</code></pre><h4 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>The tree contains at least one node.</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>Let=E2=80=99s first understand what a path is in a binary tree. An impor=
tant thing to note is that all paths have a root node. Consider the followi=
ng binary tree:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a binary tree data structure.  The root nod=
